From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 23:41:09 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_sandbox: Pause freezes the jail and Resume goes on;
 Stop asks first
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Operator report of 2026-11-23: "pausing" a thread ended it with "Agent
connection lost" and, on reconnect, "Resource not found: <session>". The
button pressed was the kill switch of 0141 (§6.53): stop_jail sends ai-jail
SIGTERM, which ends the agent and everything it started. The session had
never been prompted, so Claude Code had written no transcript in the jail's
config dir (/var/tmp/zeo-jail/<project>/claude/projects/<cwd>/ held only
memory/), and every resume answered "No conversation found" -- Claude Agent
(Plus) maps that to resourceNotFound when the session is not live in the
restarted adapter. Nothing was lost that time, but a stop is never a pause.

What the operator asked for is a pause that loses nothing, and the jail can
already do it: the disk watchdog (§6.54) freezes ai-jail's systemd scope
through cgroup.freeze and Resume thaws it. Zeo's default limits (8G memory,
CPUQuota 400%, TasksMax 4096) make ai-jail create that scope, so a frozen
jail keeps every process, its memory and its open connections, and goes on
where it stopped.

- A Pause button (DebugPause) sits beside the lock of a running jail, next to
  Stop. LaunchRegistry::pause_by_operator freezes the scope of the launch's
  ai-jail process and, only when that worked, marks the launch paused and
  remembers that the operator paused it (operator_paused, cleared on resume
  and with the launch). pause_by_operator_with takes the freeze as a
  parameter, which is what the tests replace.
- The paused controls read "Paused" for the operator's pause and keep "Paused:
  disk quota" for the watchdog's, with Resume and Stop as before; the watchdog
  already counts the quota again from a resume, whoever paused.
- Stop, beside the lock and in the paused controls, now asks "Stop the agent?"
  first: everything in the jail ends, and the prompt names Pause as the way to
  keep it.

A failed freeze (no ai-jail scope, e.g. a host without a systemd user manager)
is logged as agent_sandbox event=operator_pause outcome=failed and leaves the
jail running; nothing claims a pause.

Tests: agent_sandbox operator_pause_tests.rs -- a freeze that works marks the
launch paused by the operator and Resume clears it; a failed freeze marks
nothing; a launch with no ai-jail process freezes nothing. agent_ui
agent_jail_status_pause_tests.rs -- Pause is drawn for a running jail; an
operator pause reads "Paused", not the disk label, and offers no second
Pause; a click on Stop raises the confirmation and Cancel leaves the launch
alone (the record's pid is above any pid_max, so no real process can ever be
signalled). Red: without the UI half the three agent_ui tests fail; the
registry tests need the new API to build at all. agent_sandbox and agent_ui
suites: 1959/1959.
---

